The object this one is a replica of: `<store> <source cluster id> <source
object id>`, the store the source writes its snapshots into, as this cluster
names it, the id of the cluster of the source, and the id of the source
object.

A replica is the copy of an object another cluster runs, kept on this
cluster to take over from it in a disaster. Its `sync.store` resource of
that store follows the source instead of writing snapshots: on its
`schedule`, it takes the latest snapshot of the source, applies the
configuration it saved, and restores its heads, the instance staying
stopped.

The configuration of a replica is the one of its source, word for word.
Its instances are on the nodes of this cluster the configuration names:
its `drpnodes`, where a keyword whose value differs is scoped `@drpnodes`,
or its `nodes`, for an object a replica was promoted over. The daemon
does not start a replica on its own, and a start of it is refused unless
forced.

`deploy --store <store> --snapshot <id> --replica` sets it, and so does
`replica --of <store>` on an object a replica was promoted over. The daemon
writes it, and nobody but root changes or unsets it: unset, the object is
no longer a replica, and the daemon starts it as any object. The promotion
of the replica, `promote`, asked by an administrator of the namespace, is
what takes it away, once the store records this cluster as the primary of
the object.
